[agent] **How the work ran on the platform (CLI mode, no local compute):**
[agent] 1. Uploaded `requests.csv` / `profiles.csv` to HopsFS (`hops files upload`).
[agent] 2. Authored a PySpark feature-pipeline script and ran it as a Hopsworks job (`hops job deploy … --type pyspark --env spark-feature-pipeline --run --wait`, execution #3101 → SUCCEEDED). The job did everything platform-side: join each request to its stored profile on `account_id`, compute the on-demand transformation (`distance_deg = round(√((Δlat)²+(Δlon)²), 6)`, then `score = round(base_score − 0.1·distance_deg, 6)` using the rounded distance), and insert the 4-column result into the online-enabled feature group.
[agent] Confirmed via `hops fg info/features/stats/preview` (read back through the platform's own read paths).
[agent] Note on the interface: `hops fg create` exposes no flag to attach on-demand transformation functions to a feature group, and `fv create --transform` attaches model-dependent transforms to a *feature view* (applied at read, not stored). So the idiomatic Hopsworks "on-demand transformation attached to a feature group" wasn't expressible directly through the CLI — I computed the same logic inside a platform PySpark job instead. Also, `hops sql` (Trino) is currently unusable from the external client due to a TLS cert IP-mismatch on the Trino node; I used the Feature Query Service (`fg preview`/`fg stats`) to verify instead.
